# Copy to .env (untracked) or export these in the environment. start.sh reads
# .env, and so do the test suites, the load-test tools and the mock services
# when they are run by hand; anything already exported takes precedence.

# REQUIRED. The key the gateway signs and verifies tokens with. There is no
# default: the gateway exits at startup if this is missing or shorter than 32
# bytes. Every gateway instance behind one load balancer must share it, and
# changing it signs everyone out.
#   openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=

# REQUIRED. The networks a service may be registered in and proxied to. Both
# the registry and the gateway read it; with nothing here they refuse every
# destination. The check is made on the address a name actually resolves to,
# at connect time, so it cannot be bypassed by DNS.
#   local stack (mock services on this machine):  127.0.0.0/8,::1/128
#   a real deployment: the subnet(s) your services run in, e.g. 10.20.0.0/16
# Loopback is NOT a safe value outside development: Redis and the registry
# listen there.
UPSTREAM_ALLOWED_CIDRS=

# REQUIRED by start.sh; optional for the registry itself. The credential a
# service presents (X-Enrollment-Token) to register ITSELF with the registry.
# The registry creates a record only for an authenticated caller: this, or the
# gateway acting for a signed-in user. Give it to the registry and to the
# services meant to self-register — and nobody else. Left unset, direct
# self-registration is off and the gateway is the only way to add a service.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
REGISTRY_ENROLLMENT_TOKEN=

# Optional, further narrowing. If set, a service URL's host must match one of
# these names ("*.svc.internal" matches any subdomain) and its port must be
# listed (single ports or from-to ranges).
# UPSTREAM_ALLOWED_HOSTS=*.svc.internal
# UPSTREAM_ALLOWED_PORTS=8080,9000-9099

# Optional. Services (by name) that receive the caller's own bearer token.
# By default none do: the gateway removes the Authorization header and tells
# the service who is calling with X-Gateway-User-Id / X-Gateway-User instead.
# FORWARD_AUTHORIZATION_TO=

# Optional. How many reverse proxies sit in front of the gateway (a number), or
# the subnets they connect from ("10.0.0.0/8"). Rate limits are per client
# address; without this, behind a load balancer every client shares the
# balancer's.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# Optional. Signups allowed per client address per minute (default 300, sized
# for the test suites — set it much lower for a public deployment).
# SIGNUP_MAX_PER_MINUTE=10

# Optional. Requests one ACCOUNT may make per minute, counted per account id
# on every authenticated request: to the gateway's own API, and proxied
# through it to services. Defaults are 1200 each — about twice what the
# busiest account in the test suites uses. Set them to what your clients need;
# the load test needs them (and SIGNUP_MAX_PER_MINUTE) raised well above that.
# API_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=1200
# GATEWAY_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=1200

# Optional. The platform admin the test suites and load-test tools sign in as.
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Optional — defaults shown.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:7000
# REGISTRY_URL=http://localhost:3001
# PORT=3000

# The URL people reach the gateway at (default http://localhost:$PORT). It is
# the OpenID Connect issuer, the address in every password-recovery link, and
# the only place the console's sign-in returns to. Behind a proxy or under a
# real hostname it MUST be set, to an https:// URL.
# PUBLIC_URL=https://iam.example.org

# Optional. Enables GET /metrics (Prometheus text format) for a scraper that
# presents this as a bearer token. Unset, the endpoint answers 404. Not an
# account's token: give it to the monitoring system only.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# METRICS_TOKEN=

# Optional. Forward security logs to a central collector as they are written:
# newline-delimited JSON, POSTed in batches with the token as a bearer. The
# local files under logs/ stay the complete record. Levels default to
# audit,warn,error.
# LOG_FORWARD_URL=https://logs.example.org/ingest/iam
# LOG_FORWARD_TOKEN=
# LOG_FORWARD_LEVELS=audit,warn,error

# Optional. The notification service a self-service password recovery link is
# handed to (it knows how to reach the person; the gateway has no mail
# transport). POST {event, username, link, expiresAt} with the token as a
# bearer. Unset, "forgot my password" is recorded and answered but nothing is
# sent, and a platform admin issues recovery links instead.
# PASSWORD_RECOVERY_WEBHOOK_URL=https://notify.example.org/hooks/iam-recovery
# PASSWORD_RECOVERY_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=

# ── Backups (src/backup/redis-backup.js; `npm run backup:keygen` makes the keys) ──
# On the IAM host: the public encryption key and the signing key. With these
# it can write backups and cannot read one.
# BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE=/etc/iam/backup-encryption-public.pem
# BACKUP_SIGNING_KEY_FILE=/etc/iam/backup-signing-private.pem
# Where backups go — exactly one of these. A directory must be a mounted
# remote volume (one on the same disk as Redis's data is refused); a command
# is run through the shell with $BACKUP_FILE and $BACKUP_MANIFEST set.
# BACKUP_DIR=/mnt/backup-nas/iam
# BACKUP_UPLOAD_COMMAND=aws s3 cp "$BACKUP_FILE" s3://backups/iam/ && aws s3 cp "$BACKUP_MANIFEST" s3://backups/iam/
# BACKUP_RETENTION_COUNT=30
# On the RESTORE host only — never here: the decryption key (and its
# passphrase) and the verification key, for `verify`, `decrypt` and `drill`.
# BACKUP_DECRYPTION_KEY_FILE=
# BACKUP_VERIFICATION_KEY_FILE=
# BACKUP_KEY_PASSPHRASE=

# Where the one-time password of the initial 'admin' account is written the
# first time the gateway boots against an empty Redis (mode 0600, removed once
# that password has been changed).
# INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD_FILE=.run/initial-admin-password

# Where a self-registering service keeps the registry token that proves it
# owns its name (one file per service, mode 0600).
# SERVICE_TOKEN_DIR=.run/service-tokens
